### Conflict Detection — The Critical Moment

```
CONFLICT CHECK F6 vs F4:
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

TUPLE COMPARISON:
  F6: (file, max_size, lte(5_GB),    when(customer_type=enterprise))
  F4: (file, max_size, lte(100_MB),  none)

CONFLICT EQUATION:
  entity match?      file == file               ✅ YES
  property match?    max_size == max_size        ✅ YES
  value mismatch?    5_GB ≠ 100_MB              ✅ YES (5120 MB ≠ 100 MB)
  condition overlap? enterprise ∩ none = ???      ⚠️ NEEDS ANALYSIS

CONDITION OVERLAP ANALYSIS:
  F4 condition: none (unconditional — applies to ALL users)
  F6 condition: when(customer_type=enterprise)

  Does "enterprise" overlap with "all users"?

  Enterprise customers ARE a subset of all users.
  Therefore: enterprise ∩ all_users = enterprise ≠ ∅

  The conditions DO overlap. For enterprise customers,
  BOTH specs would apply simultaneously:
    F4 says: max_size ≤ 100 MB
    F6 says: max_size ≤ 5 GB

  These give different answers for the same user type.

  HOWEVER, there's a subtlety:

  INTERPRETATION A: F4 is the DEFAULT, F6 is an OVERRIDE for enterprise
    → These are compatible if F6 replaces F4 for enterprise users
    → This is what Carol likely INTENDS
    → But F4 says "no larger than 100 megabytes" with NO exception mentioned

  INTERPRETATION B: F4 is ABSOLUTE, F6 contradicts it
    → F4 sets a hard limit, F6 violates that limit
    → These are genuinely incompatible

  The system CANNOT determine which interpretation is correct.
  This is EXACTLY the kind of ambiguity the SDD-Compiler must flag.

VERDICT: ❌ CONFLICT DETECTED

  Conflict record:
  {
    id: "C1",
    node_a: "F4",
    node_b: "F6",
    reason: "Both specify max_size for file entity. F4 sets
             unconditional limit of 100MB. F6 sets 5GB limit
             for enterprise customers. Enterprise customers
             are a subset of all users, so both rules apply
             to them simultaneously with different values.
             Unclear whether F6 is meant to override F4 or
             if these are incompatible requirements.",
    severity: "hard",
    resolved: false
  }

NODE STATUS UPDATES:
  F4: status changed from "validated" → "conflict"
  F6: status = "conflict" (new node, born in conflict)
```

```
DOWNSTREAM IMPACT ANALYSIS:
════════════════════════════

The conflict between F4 and F6 affects downstream nodes:

  F4 constrains → A1 (S3 storage)
  If max_size changes from 100MB to 5GB, does this impact
  S3 configuration?

  Analysis: S3 supports objects up to 5TB. A 5GB file is
  within S3 limits. However:
  - S3 requires multipart upload for files > 5GB (so 5GB is
    at the boundary)
  - Upload timeout configurations may need adjustment
  - Storage cost projections change significantly

  A1 impact: ⚠️ AMBIGUOUS (may need configuration changes)
  → Mark A1 as status "ambiguous"

  F4 also constrains → F1 (upload capability)
  If max_size changes, upload UI/UX may need to change
  (progress bar, timeout messaging, etc.)

  F1 impact: ⚠️ AMBIGUOUS
```

