## Generalized Complexity: Adding the (n+1)th Node

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
BEST CASE, WORST CASE, AVERAGE CASE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Given: n existing nodes, adding 1 new node
       K = top-K candidates for conflict check (typically 5-10)
       a = number of anchor nodes for BFS
       b = average branching factor of graph
       d = max BFS depth (typically 2)
       F = number of families
       L = number of layers (fixed at 7)
       D = embedding dimension (768 or 1536)


BEST CASE: New node has NO entity matches, unique topic
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Signal 1: O(e) entity lookups where e = entities in new node
            Result: all zeros (no matches)
            → 0 anchors for Signal 3

  Signal 2: O(n × D) for cosine similarities
            Result: all low scores

  Signal 3: O(n) for anchor check, 0 BFS (no anchors)

  Signal 4: O(n) for layer scoring

  Fusion:   O(n log n) sort

  Conflict: O(0) — nothing above threshold

  BEST CASE TOTAL: O(n × D) dominated by embedding

  For n=13, D=768:  ~10,000 FLOPs + ~50 logical ops
  For n=1000, D=768: ~768,000 FLOPs + ~4,000 logical ops

  This is the "lonely node" case — new node is in a
  completely novel domain with no connections.


WORST CASE: New node entity-matches EVERY existing node
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Signal 1: O(n) — every node scores 1.0
            → potentially n anchors for Signal 3

  Signal 2: O(n × D) — all cosines computed

  Signal 3: O(n) anchor checks → many anchors
            BFS from each anchor: O(b^d) per anchor
            WORST: O(n × b^d) if every node is an anchor
            With b=4, d=2: O(n × 16) = O(16n)

            BUT: BFS visits are deduplicated (visited set)
            True worst case: O(n + E) where E = total edges
            Since E ≤ n² in worst case: O(n²)
            In practice E ≈ 2n to 5n: O(n)

  Signal 4: O(n)

  Fusion:   O(n log n) sort + O(n) scoring

  Conflict: O(K) — but K is capped!
            Even if all nodes are related, we only check
            top K = 10 for conflicts.
            Each conflict check: O(1) tuple comparison
            + occasionally O(1) LLM call

  WORST CASE TOTAL: O(n × D + n log n + K)
                    ≈ O(n × D)    (embedding dominates)

  For n=13, D=768:    ~15,000 FLOPs + ~400 logical ops
  For n=1000, D=768:  ~800,000 FLOPs + ~15,000 logical ops
  For n=10000, D=768: ~8,000,000 FLOPs + ~150,000 logical ops

  Note: 8M FLOPs is trivial for modern hardware.
  A laptop CPU does ~100 GFLOPS = 100,000,000,000 FLOPs/second.
  8M FLOPs takes 0.00008 seconds.

  THE BOTTLENECK IS NEVER THE MATH. IT'S THE LLM CALLS.


AVERAGE CASE: Typical product spec
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Typical parameters:
    Entity matches: ~30% of nodes (0.3n)
    Anchors: 2-5
    BFS branching: 3-4, depth: 2
    Top-K: 5-10
    LLM calls: 0-2 (for ambiguous cases)

  Signal 1: O(n) scoring, ~0.3n matches
  Signal 2: O(n × D)
  Signal 3: O(n) anchor check + O(a × b²) BFS
            = O(n + 5 × 16) = O(n + 80) ≈ O(n)
  Signal 4: O(n)
  Fusion:   O(n log n)
  Conflict: O(K) ≈ O(10)
  LLM:      0-2 calls × ~1-3 seconds each

  AVERAGE CASE TOTAL: O(n × D + n log n)
                      ≈ O(n × D)

  Wall clock time (n=100, D=768):
    Math:     ~0.001 seconds
    LLM calls: ~2-6 seconds
    Total:    ~2-6 seconds (LLM dominated)

  Wall clock time (n=1000, D=768):
    Math:     ~0.01 seconds
    LLM calls: ~2-6 seconds
    Total:    ~2-6 seconds (STILL LLM dominated)
```

