## Part 9: Summary — The Engineering Tradeoffs

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
FINAL VERDICT
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

FOR SMALL SPECS (< 30 requirements):
  Use flat markdown. It's simpler, cheaper, and good enough.
  The model can attend to all specs simultaneously.
  Coverage is adequate (~70-80%).
  Token cost: lower.

FOR MEDIUM SPECS (30-100 requirements):
  Graph becomes advantageous. Flat markdown coverage drops
  to 40-55%. Graph maintains 90%+.
  Token cost: ~30% higher initially, but LOWER per correct spec.
  Regeneration savings: ~70%.

FOR LARGE SPECS (100+ requirements):
  Graph is NECESSARY. Flat markdown coverage drops below 40%.
  The model cannot effectively attend to 100+ specs while
  generating code.
  Token cost: ~20% higher initially, but 4-5× more efficient
  per correctly implemented spec.
  Regeneration is DRAMATICALLY cheaper.

THE COUNTERINTUITIVE INSIGHT:
  The graph costs more tokens UPFRONT but saves tokens OVERALL
  because:
  1. Higher first-pass accuracy → fewer regeneration cycles
  2. Targeted regeneration → fix only what's broken
  3. Cross-file consistency → fewer integration bugs
  4. Completeness tracking → nothing is forgotten

  Total lifecycle token cost (generation + all fixes):

  n specs    Flat Total    Graph Total    Graph Advantage
  ──────     ──────────    ───────────    ───────────────
  14          ~9,000        ~8,200        1.1× cheaper
  50         ~30,000       ~20,000        1.5× cheaper
  100        ~60,000       ~35,000        1.7× cheaper
  500       ~250,000      ~140,000        1.8× cheaper

  THE GRAPH APPROACH IS CHEAPER IN TOTAL LIFECYCLE COST
  FOR ANY PROJECT WITH MORE THAN ~25 SPECIFICATIONS.
```

```
THE ONE-SENTENCE SUMMARY:

  Flat markdown is cheaper to START but expensive to FINISH.
  Graph-based is expensive to START but cheap to FINISH.

  Since software development is mostly FINISHING (iterating,
  fixing, refining), the graph wins on total cost.
```

