## Part 2: Local vs LLM — The Hybrid Architecture

### Hardware Capability Assessment

```
YOUR MACHINE:
═════════════

  CPU:  Strong (assumed modern 8+ core)
  GPU:  RTX 5070 (~12GB VRAM, CUDA cores ~7000+)
  RAM:  16GB system memory

WHAT CAN THIS HARDWARE RUN?

  ┌──────────────────────────────────┬──────────┬────────────────┐
  │ MODEL TYPE                       │ VRAM     │ FEASIBLE?      │
  ├──────────────────────────────────┼──────────┼────────────────┤
  │ Embedding model (all-MiniLM-L6)  │ ~0.5 GB  │ ✅ Easily       │
  │ Embedding model (BGE-large)      │ ~1.5 GB  │ ✅ Easily       │
  │ Embedding model (E5-large-v2)    │ ~1.3 GB  │ ✅ Easily       │
  │ 7B LLM (Llama 3 Q4 quantized)   │ ~4 GB    │ ✅ Yes          │
  │ 7B LLM (Llama 3 Q8 quantized)   │ ~7 GB    │ ✅ Yes          │
  │ 13B LLM (Q4 quantized)          │ ~8 GB    │ ✅ Tight but ok │
  │ 14B LLM (Qwen2.5-Coder-14B Q4)  │ ~9 GB    │ ⚠️ Tight        │
  │ 34B LLM (Q4 quantized)          │ ~20 GB   │ ❌ Too large    │
  │ 70B LLM (Q4 quantized)          │ ~40 GB   │ ❌ Way too large│
  │ FAISS index (100K vectors 768D)  │ ~0.3 GB  │ ✅ Trivial      │
  │ Graph algorithms (NetworkX)      │ ~0 GPU   │ ✅ CPU only     │
  │ Rule engine / pattern matching   │ ~0 GPU   │ ✅ CPU only     │
  │ JSON/text processing             │ ~0 GPU   │ ✅ CPU only     │
  │ NER model (spaCy/small)          │ ~0.2 GB  │ ✅ Easily       │
  │ Classifier (fine-tuned BERT)     │ ~0.5 GB  │ ✅ Easily       │
  └──────────────────────────────────┴──────────┴────────────────┘

KEY INSIGHT:
  Your RTX 5070 can run a 7B model + embedding model + FAISS
  simultaneously with room to spare.

  ~4GB (7B LLM) + ~1.5GB (embedding) + ~0.3GB (FAISS) = ~6GB
  Remaining VRAM: ~6GB free for batch processing
```

### Mapping Operations to Hardware

```
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
OPERATION-BY-OPERATION: LOCAL vs LLM
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


OPERATION 1: ATOMIZATION (text → atomic nodes)
──────────────────────────────────────────────

  SUB-TASK 1A: Clause Segmentation
    Break sentences into independent clauses.

    LOCAL APPROACH:
      Tool: spaCy dependency parser
      Method: Parse sentence tree, split at coordinating
              conjunctions and relative clauses
      VRAM: ~0.2 GB (spaCy small/medium model)
      Speed: ~1000 sentences/second
      Accuracy: ~85% on clean text, ~70% on messy text

    LLM APPROACH:
      Accuracy: ~95%
      Cost: ~200 tokens per spec paragraph

    VERDICT: ⚠️ LOCAL IS VIABLE BUT WORSE
      For a POC/hackathon: use LLM
      For production: use local with LLM fallback for
      complex sentences

    TOKEN SAVINGS: ~200 tokens per spec if done locally

  SUB-TASK 1B: Entity Extraction
    Identify technical entities (file, share_link, token, etc.)

    LOCAL APPROACH:
      Tool: Fine-tuned NER model or spaCy with custom patterns
      Method: Train on software domain entities OR use
              rule-based pattern matching

      Rule-based extraction patterns:
        ENTITY_PATTERNS = [
          r"(\w+)\s+(service|module|handler|controller)",
          r"(\w+)\s+(table|entity|model|schema)",
          r"(\w+)\s+(endpoint|API|route)",
          r"(\w+)\s+(token|key|credential|secret)",
          r"(file|user|session|account|link|upload|download)"
        ]

      VRAM: 0 GB (pure regex) or ~0.5 GB (NER model)
      Speed: ~5000 sentences/second (regex), ~500/sec (NER)
      Accuracy: ~80% (regex), ~88% (NER)

    LLM APPROACH:
      Accuracy: ~95%

    VERDICT: ✅ LOCAL IS GOOD ENOUGH
      Entity extraction is a well-solved NLP problem.
      Domain-specific patterns + small NER model is reliable.

    TOKEN SAVINGS: Embedded in the atomization call,
    ~100 tokens if separated

  SUB-TASK 1C: Tuple Decomposition (Entity, Property, Constraint, Condition)

    LOCAL APPROACH:
      Tool: Fine-tuned sequence-to-sequence model OR
            dependency parsing + rules

      This is the HARDEST sub-task to do locally because
      it requires UNDERSTANDING what the spec means, not
      just parsing its syntax.

      Example that breaks rule-based approaches:
        "The system should be fast"
        → Entity: system
        → Property: performance
        → Constraint: fast (but what does "fast" mean?)
        → This requires SEMANTIC understanding.

      A 7B model COULD do this but with ~75% accuracy.

    LLM APPROACH:
      Accuracy: ~92%
      Cost: ~300 tokens per spec paragraph

    VERDICT: ❌ USE LLM (for now)
      Tuple decomposition requires semantic understanding
      that small local models can't reliably provide.
      This is the CORE VALUE of the LLM in the pipeline.

    BUT: If you have consistent spec patterns (your team
    writes specs similarly each time), a fine-tuned 7B
    model could approach ~85% accuracy.


OPERATION 2: PLACEMENT (layer classification + family assignment)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  SUB-TASK 2A: Layer Classification

    LOCAL APPROACH (CASCADE):

      Level 1 — REGEX RULES (cost: zero):
        import re

        LAYER_PATTERNS = {
          'L0': [r'business\s+need', r'compliance', r'market',
                 r'revenue', r'user\s+need'],
          'L1': [r'user\s+(can|should|must)', r'system\s+(shall|must)',
                 r'when\s+.+\s+then', r'display', r'notif'],
          'L2': [r'microservice', r'service', r'module', r'component',
                 r'database', r'queue', r'cache', r'S3|Redis|Kafka'],
          'L3': [r'(GET|POST|PUT|DELETE|PATCH)\s+/', r'endpoint',
                 r'API', r'schema', r'request|response', r'header'],
          'L4': [r'table', r'entity', r'field', r'column', r'foreign\s+key',
                 r'index', r'nullable', r'varchar|integer|boolean'],
          'L5': [r'if\s+.+\s+then', r'calculate', r'algorithm',
                 r'validate\s+by', r'hash', r'encrypt', r'iterate'],
          'L6': [r'test:', r'verify\s+that', r'assert', r'expect',
                 r'given.+when.+then', r'should\s+return']
        }

        VRAM: 0 GB
        Speed: ~100,000 classifications/second
        Accuracy: ~65%

      Level 2 — EMBEDDING CLASSIFIER (cost: minimal GPU):
        Pre-computed layer centroids (7 vectors of dimension 768)
        Compute embedding of new node
        Nearest centroid = classified layer

        VRAM: ~0.5 GB for embedding model
        Speed: ~1,000 classifications/second
        Accuracy: ~80%

      Level 3 — LOCAL 7B MODEL (cost: moderate GPU):
        Use Llama-3-8B or Qwen2.5-7B with a classification prompt

        VRAM: ~4 GB (Q4 quantized)
        Speed: ~5-10 classifications/second
        Accuracy: ~88%

      COMBINED CASCADE:
        70% resolved at Level 1 → 0 tokens
        15% resolved at Level 2 → 0 tokens
        15% resolved at Level 3 → 0 tokens (local model)

    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% — NO API TOKENS NEEDED ✅

    VERDICT: ✅ FULLY LOCAL
      Layer classification is a 7-class classification problem.
      This is well within local model capabilities.

  SUB-TASK 2B: Family Assignment

    LOCAL APPROACH:
      Entity-based: Dictionary lookup → O(1), 0 VRAM
      Embedding-based: Cosine similarity to family centroids → 0.5 GB
      New family naming: Local 7B model → 4 GB

    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% — NO API TOKENS NEEDED ✅

    VERDICT: ✅ FULLY LOCAL


OPERATION 3: LINKING (finding related nodes)
────────────────────────────────────────────

  Signal 1 — ENTITY MATCH:
    100% local. Inverted index lookup. Pure data structure.
    VRAM: 0 GB (CPU RAM only)
    Speed: O(1) per lookup
    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅

  Signal 2 — EMBEDDING SIMILARITY:
    100% local. Pre-compute embeddings, cosine similarity.
    VRAM: ~0.5 GB for embedding model + ~0.3 GB for FAISS index
    Speed: ~10,000 comparisons/second (brute force)
           ~100,000 comparisons/second (FAISS HNSW)
    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅

  Signal 3 — GRAPH PROXIMITY:
    100% local. BFS traversal on adjacency list.
    VRAM: 0 GB (CPU only)
    Speed: O(b^d) per anchor, microseconds
    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅

  Signal 4 — LAYER PATTERN:
    100% local. Lookup table.
    VRAM: 0 GB
    Speed: O(1) per node
    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅

  SCORE FUSION:
    100% local. Arithmetic operations.
    TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅


OPERATION 4: CONFLICT DETECTION
──────────────────────────────

  SUB-TASK 4A: Tuple-Based Conflict Check
    Compare (entity, property, constraint, condition) tuples.

    IF tuples are well-formed (from Operation 1):
      100% local. Field comparison.
      VRAM: 0 GB
      Speed: O(1) per pair
      TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅

    BUT: Tuple quality depends on Operation 1 (atomization),
    which uses LLM. Garbage in → garbage out.

  SUB-TASK 4B: Semantic Conflict Verification
    For ambiguous cases where tuples don't clearly match
    but semantic reasoning suggests a potential conflict.

    "Signed URLs expire after 1 hour" vs "Share links valid 30 days"
    → Tuples have different entities (signed_url vs share_link)
    → But semantically these MIGHT be the same thing

    LOCAL APPROACH:
      7B model with conflict detection prompt
      VRAM: ~4 GB
      Speed: ~2-5 seconds per pair
      Accuracy: ~70-75% (7B models struggle with nuanced reasoning)

    LLM APPROACH:
      Accuracy: ~90%
      Cost: ~200 tokens per ambiguous pair

    VERDICT: ⚠️ HYBRID
      Use local for CLEAR conflicts (tuple match → automatic)
      Use LLM for AMBIGUOUS conflicts (semantic reasoning)

      Typically: 80% of conflicts are clear → local
                 20% require semantic reasoning → LLM

    TOKEN SAVINGS: ~80%


OPERATION 5: TASK GROUPING
────────────────────────

  100% local. Graph partitioning algorithm.
  VRAM: 0 GB (CPU only, NetworkX)
  Speed: O(n^1.5), milliseconds
  TOKEN SAVINGS: 100% ✅


OPERATION 6: CODE GENERATION
──────────────────────────

  CANNOT be done locally with a 7B model.

  WHY NOT?

  Code generation requires:
  - Understanding multiple specs simultaneously
  - Synthesizing coherent, production-quality code
  - Maintaining consistency across a file
  - Following language idioms and best practices
  - Handling edge cases correctly

  7B models CAN generate code, but quality is significantly
  lower than frontier models (Claude, GPT-4, etc.):

    Task                    7B Local    Frontier LLM
    ─────────────────       ────────    ────────────
    Simple functions        ~75%        ~95%
    Multi-file modules      ~45%        ~85%
    Complex logic           ~30%        ~75%
    Edge case handling      ~20%        ~60%
    Architecture coherence  ~35%        ~80%

  A 14B coding-specific model (Qwen2.5-Coder-14B) on your
  RTX 5070 would perform better:
    Simple functions:       ~85%
    Multi-file modules:     ~60%
    Complex logic:          ~50%

  But still significantly below frontier models for the
  SYNTHESIS task of turning specs into coherent code.

  VERDICT: ❌ USE LLM API for code generation

  This is the ONE operation where the LLM API is essential
  and irreplaceable with current hardware.
```

### The Hybrid Architecture

